[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Workspace Management, File Operations, and Artifact Archiving in Jenkins"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Efficient workspace management and artifact archiving are critical components of a robust CI/CD pipeline. In Jenkins, managing how files are handled, cleaned, and preserved ensures predictable builds and seamless handoffs between pipeline stages."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"1. Understanding the Jenkins Workspace"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Jenkins workspace is a dedicated directory allocated on the master or agent node where a job executes. It contains all checked-out source code, generated build artifacts, and intermediate files needed during execution."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"2. Cleaning Workspaces"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"Accumulated files from previous builds can cause flaky test results or contaminate build outputs. Using the Workspace Cleanup plugin allows you to wipe the workspace clean before or after a build."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Clean Workspace') {
            steps {
                cleanWs()
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"3. Essential File Operations"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Jenkins Pipeline provides built-in steps for common file operations such as copying, moving, deleting, and reading files directly within the workspace."},{"order":10,"type":"bullet_point","text":"readFile: Reads text files from the workspace into a pipeline variable."},{"order":11,"type":"bullet_point","text":"writeFile: Writes string data directly to a specified file path."},{"order":12,"type":"bullet_point","text":"fileExists: Checks if a specific file or directory exists before proceeding."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"node {
    stage('File Operations') {
        // Write configuration
        writeFile file: 'config.json', text: '{"version": "1.0.0"}'

        // Check existence and read
        if (fileExists('config.json')) {
            def content = readFile 'config.json'
            echo "Read configuration: ${content}"
        }
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"subheading","text":"4. Archiving Artifacts"},{"order":15,"type":"paragraph","text":"Once a build produces deployable outputs (such as JAR, WAR, or ZIP files), you should archive them so they are preserved alongside the build history. The archiveArtifacts step saves these files to the Jenkins master controller."},{"order":16,"type":"code","text":"pipeline {
    agent any
    stages {
        stage('Build and Archive') {
            steps {
                sh 'mvn clean package'
                archiveArtifacts artifacts: 'target/*.jar', fingerprint: true
            }
        }
    }
}"},{"order":17,"type":"subheading","text":"5. Best Practices"},{"order":18,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Only archive necessary binaries and reports to conserve disk space on the Jenkins controller."},{"order":19,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always clean up temporary files using cleanWs() at the start or end of a pipeline."},{"order":20,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Use fingerprinting on critical artifacts to track which jobs consumed or produced specific versions."}]
